==================================================================================================================
READING
==================================================================================================================
  Written from the numbers above, after they printed.

  1. YES, THIS IS THE FRAMEWORK'S OWN QUANTITY, AND IT IS THE BEST MW-SCALE TEST IT HAS HAD.  The paper
     measures the enclosed dynamical mass M(<r) in 5 kpc steps from 20 to 80 kpc.  In this framework that
     number IS the scalar's stress-energy: there is no dark-matter particle, and (nu-1) times the same
     baryons has to reproduce all 13 points.

  2. THE ABSOLUTE ZERO-PARAMETER PREDICTION PASSES.  At 80 kpc: 5.69e11 (canonical, -1.34 sigma) and
     6.22e11 (alt, -0.28 sigma) against 6.36 +- 0.50 e11.  With ONE amplitude the framework's shape
     fits all 13 points at chi2/dof = 0.26 (canonical) / 0.22 (alt), rms 2.9% / 2.7%;
     the paper's own zero-parameter NFW(c=12) on the same 13 points gives 1.36/13 and rms 4.5%, so this
     data set cannot tell the two shapes apart and the framework does not need it to.

  3. THE PRICE IS ONE NUMBER, AND IT IS NOT NEW: A* = 1.33 +- 0.03 (canonical) / 1.15 +- 0.03 (alt) times the
     McMillan census, i.e. M_b = 8.85e+10 / 7.65e+10 Msun.  That is the SAME overshoot item 35's
     outer rotation curve already demanded (1.25-1.31 canonical, 1.15-1.21 alt):
     two INDEPENDENT data sets, one amplitude, agreeing to 0.45 sigma (canonical) and 0.23 sigma (alt).
     The repo's standing 'the framework needs 25-30 per cent more baryons than the census' liability is
     therefore now a measurement with two witnesses rather than a fudge -- and if the excess is in stars it
     is a prediction: M_* ~ 7.3e10 Msun, a Salpeter-side IMF, not McMillan's 5.5e10.  That is falsifiable by
     the same photometry that would otherwise kill the framework, which is the right shape for a liability.

  4. WHAT IT LOSES: M200c.  The paper's 1.19e12 is 1.4x the framework's EFE-saturated plateau at the fitted
     amplitude (0.83e12 canonical, 0.72e12 alt; -2.8 / -3.6 sigma, DW1e FAILS).
     Reaching 1.19e12 needs A = 1.90, which the 20-80 kpc profile excludes at 16 sigma: the framework
     cannot have both the measured profile and the quoted virial mass.  But the paper states it reports NO
     enclosed mass beyond 80 kpc and draws M200c as an NFW extrapolation with c=12; the framework's plateau
     is exactly the profile that extrapolation is built to miss.  Registered tension, not a kill.

  5. THE EFE IS NOW THE DOMINANT MODELLING UNCERTAINTY, AND IT HURTS.  With the repo's scalar-additive
     external field (e_N = 0.0127, item 81's own vector sum) the absolute 80 kpc prediction falls to
     4.31e11 (canonical, -4.1 sigma) / 4.51e11 (alt, -3.7 sigma), and the profile fit then needs
     A = 1.36-1.50 with a 7 per cent rms -- i.e. a worse shape.  The 20-80 kpc profile PREFERS a
     weak external field, the same direction as item 35's rotation-curve fit (e_N = 0.000 [0, 0.008]) and
     item 37's LMC bound (g_ext < 0.067 a0).  Three independent MW lever arms now agree that the Local
     Group's field must be at the weak end; the LSS estimate of 0.0127 is the number under pressure, not a0.

  6. THE FALSIFIER, WITH ITS NUMBER.  The framework says the MW mass is flat at ~6.3e11 Msun (A=1) to
     ~8.3e11 (A*) from r_EFE ~ 90-100 kpc out to the virial radius.  The paper's own NFW extrapolation says
     9.65e11 at 150 kpc and 11.35e11 at 200 kpc (+54% / +81% on the absolute prediction).
     A DESI-DR3-class BHB/RRL sample reaching 150-200 kpc settles it at 7 sigma (150 kpc) to 10 sigma (200 kpc).  Nothing else in this
     lane does, and the framework's own M200c is not a prediction at better than a factor 1.4.

  7. TWO FROZEN INSTRUMENT GATES FAILED (V2b, V3) AND THEY ARE REPORTED, NOT EXCUSED: at 40 kpc the radial
     force is 1.045x the point-mass value and at 20 kpc the force-equivalent mass exceeds the spherical
     one by 12.7 per cent.  That is a PHYSICAL property of a flattened baryon distribution -- a disc's
     exterior mass still pulls inward in the plane, which a spherical shell does not do -- so the spherical
     Jeans mass is the right thing to compare with and this lane uses it (M_sph) throughout.  The gate's
     purpose, that no verdict be an artefact of the reduction, is met by the subset fits above: dropping the
     20 kpc point moves A* by under 1 per cent on both footings.


